ops/rebuild-2026-09-29/codex/task-1.1-brief-transcript.log:4874:+**Decision proposed for Claude:** do not create a shared workspace or a root path dependency in Task 1.1. The root `Cargo.toml` has no `[workspace]`, no engine dependency, and root `src/` has no engine consumer. A nested independent Cargo package needs neither a root workspace entry nor a root dependency. Its own lockfile and explicit `--manifest-path` commands give a separate feature resolution. Keep root manifest/lock/source byte-identical.
ops/rebuild-2026-09-29/codex/task-1.1-brief-transcript.log:4876:+Task 1.2 lives inside the engine package. Task 1.3's planned `src/kaggriculture/observe.rs` may become the first root consumer when compiled against `Game`; Task 1.4 certainly will. Add the path dependency at the **first actual compiled consumer**, after reviewing that task, rather than claiming nothing before 1.4 can need it. There is no consumer that justifies it in **this** task.
ops/rebuild-2026-09-29/codex/task-1.1-brief-transcript.log:4878:+Workspace membership is not a dependency edge, but it is also **not feature isolation**. Cargo's locally installed primary documentation (`share/doc/rust/html/cargo/reference/resolver.html`, “Features”, in `nightly-2026-04-18-aarch64-apple-darwin`) explicitly states that selecting multiple workspace packages with `--workspace` or multiple `-p` flags unifies dependency features; separate invocations avoid that unification. A shared workspace also shares lock resolution. Resolver 2/3 does not isolate a normal engine dependency from the root's active test dependency on the same `serde_json` package. A root-only default invocation can avoid the engine when no dependency selects it, but `cargo test --workspace` would reintroduce L4.
ops/rebuild-2026-09-29/codex/task-1.1-brief-transcript.log:4880:+Keep engine `serde_json` features `arbitrary_precision` and `preserve_order`: removing them breaks the kernel's tested unbounded integer/numeric and ordered-inventory semantics. If Claude chooses a shared workspace now, the minimal way to keep unmodified root decoding is **separate package test invocations** (never selecting both); the more robust choice is the standalone package proposed here. Once the root needs the path dependency, apply the reference's narrow test-only repair, not feature deletion:
ops/rebuild-2026-09-29/codex/task-1.1-brief-transcript.log:4906:+- All **25 registry packages** in the reference engine lockfile are present in the local Cargo cache. A temporary copy of its unchanged `Cargo.toml`/`Cargo.lock` passed `cargo metadata --offline --locked --format-version 1` (26 packages including the engine). No missing engine crates, downloads, or engine compilation. Metadata resolution is not proof of compilation.
